tgs:Concept.AIAsMetacrisisAccelerant thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.ActorlessThreats thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RodSchoonover .

tgs:Concept.AdvancePolicy thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NateHagens,
        tgs:Persona.RichardGephardt ;
    skos:note "The project Nate Hagens describes (TGS_5) with former US House Majority Leader Dick Gephardt: preparing 'interventions and plans and blueprints that our society are going to need in the next decade, but that are politically or socially impossible to happen now' -- doing the research and building constituencies for them in advance, so they are ready when conditions change. It answers the observation that a system dependent on debt, central-bank support and deficit spending will not adopt measures such as a tax on non-renewable inputs until a crisis forces it. Nate's bend-versus-break framing calls these the 'blueprints and airbags' whose absence makes a break more likely."@en .

tgs:Concept.AerosolMasking skos:note "The cooling effect of air pollution, chiefly sulfur dioxide, which reflects sunlight directly and seeds bright low clouds, hiding part of the warming greenhouse gases have already caused; cleaning up the pollution 'unmasks' that warming. In TGS_236 Zeke Hausfather says aerosols have masked roughly a third of historical warming (the IPCC best estimate is about 0.5°C, range 0.22-0.96°C), that global SO2 emissions are down about 40%, China's 75% and shipping's about 80% after the 2020 IMO low-sulfur fuel rule, and that in his estimate nearly all the recent acceleration in warming, from about 0.2 to about 0.3°C per decade, comes from these cuts. Shipping's cuts matter more than their share because ships pollute otherwise clean marine air. Because high-sensitivity models need high masking to match observations, the size of the effect is tied to how much future warming to expect. He still prefers clean air, given millions of premature deaths a year from air pollution."@en .

