tgs:Concept.ComplexSystemsThinking skos:note "The general toolkit of concepts Hagens draws on for reasoning about civilizational dynamics: systems move in fits and starts rather than smoothly; ecological phase shifts exhibit path dependency (once crossed, a threshold is not easily uncrossed); non-linear causality and feedback loops mean small inputs can produce disproportionate effects; and real tipping points (ice sheets, soil systems, per the Global Tipping Points Report 2025) can be crossed well before they become visible in conventional metrics."@en .

tgs:Concept.ComplexityCollapse thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.JosephTainter .

tgs:Concept.ConstructiveJournalism thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.MarenUrner .

tgs:Concept.ContextBasedSustainability thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Organization.R30 .

tgs:Concept.ContinuityValueOfProvision thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.IndyJohar .

tgs:Concept.CorrelationOfForceAndMeans thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.CostConvenienceComfort thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.WesCarter .

tgs:Concept.CostOfExtractionPricing thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    skos:note "Nate Hagens's critique (TGS_5), which Daniel Schmachtenberger calls 'core to what's wrong with economic theory': fossil hydrocarbons are a one-time endowment drawn down about 10 million times faster than they were sequestered, yet the economy 'is treating this as if it were interest when it actually is our principal.' A barrel of oil does about five years of human labor, but its price is only the cost of getting it out of the ground plus a margin -- not the cost of making more hydrocarbons, nor of the pollution, extraction damage or wars over its location. So 'the prices of everything are wrong': if the full, wide-boundary externalities of energy use were included, no industry on Earth would be profitable. Daniel's restatement: the market as a whole is being subsidized by nature from a savings account that is about to run out, and when it does, costs rise enough to force a restructuring -- the break scenario."@en .

tgs:Concept.CoupledSystemicRisks thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.IndyJohar .

tgs:Concept.CulturalMitochondria skos:note "Nate's own real, coined framework, laid out in full in Frankly_98, for ten personal traits he speculates could seed a genuinely more resilient and cooperative culture after the economic Superorganism's own eventual splintering — framed directly as 'cultural mitochondria,' small, distributed, largely invisible units of capacity, extending Marvin Harris's own real cultural-materialism pyramid (infrastructure, structure, superstructure, already built) to the real inner world of individuals the original framework left out. Nate's own real, numbered list, given deliberately 'in no particular order' despite the numbering: (0) self-care as a real neurobiological precondition, not indulgence; (1) grounding, staying tethered to body and breath; (2) a post-tragic mindset, metabolizing grief into daily rhythm rather than avoiding it; (3) being networked, decentralized and mycelial rather than hierarchical, 'finding the others'; (4) co-regulation with others via real polyvagal safety, not just individual regulation; (5) systems thinking / wide boundary thinking; (6) restraint, deliberately saying no to consumption and supernormal stimuli; (7) being status-free or status-aware rather than status-driven; (8) genuinely feeling interconnection with the web of life, not just knowing it intellectually; (9) embracing emergence over fixed outcomes, seeding present conditions rather than planning a final result; and (10) play and joy, explicitly named optional yet real and important."@en .

tgs:Concept.CustodianshipOverOwnership thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.IndyJohar .

tgs:Concept.DarkRetreat thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.AndrewHolecek .

tgs:Concept.DarknessDeficitDisorder thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.AndrewHolecek .

tgs:Concept.DefaultModeNetwork skos:note "The brain-region network active during self-referential processing, theory of mind, mind-wandering, and mental time travel — anti-correlated with the task-positive network (TPN), which handles direct engaged attention. Chronic DMN dominance is measurably linked to reduced hippocampal grey matter, higher anxiety/depression, and diminished sensory processing."@en .

tgs:Concept.DenialLearnedInDominationFamilies thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RianeEisler .

tgs:Concept.DieselPoweredCivilization thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.ArtBerman,
        tgs:Persona.BalazsMatics .

tgs:Concept.DiningCar thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NateHagens .

tgs:Concept.DiscountRatesAndPresentBias thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.MarcusAurelius .

tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency skos:note "A real, recurring pattern across independently-recorded episodes: meaningful change comes from ordinary, distributed action rather than a singular hero or savior figure. Direct textual evidence spans multiple episodes, not one coined term: Frankly_58's own real closing reframe (Sam and Gollum, not Frodo, as the actual Catalysts who changed Lord of the Rings' outcome — 'you never know when your actions could shift the larger story'); Frankly_132's direct claim that reluctance to lead 'might be a good indication that you could be a good leader tomorrow,' paired with its critique that modern systems select for Dark Triad traits in those who actively seek leadership; RR-17's two guests independently converging on 'find like-minded people close to home' as their practical closing advice, rather than pointing toward a movement or a leader; and the real, structural logic of Buckminster Fuller's trim-tab principle (small, precisely-placed, unglamorous leverage redirecting something enormous), which is also the real source of Priscilla Trịnh's own 'Trintab' nickname from RR-9."@en .

tgs:Concept.DividedBrain thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.IainMcGilchrist ;
    skos:note "Iain McGilchrist's account, set out in The Master and His Emissary (2009) and The Matter with Things (2021), that the brain's two hemispheres differ not in what they do (logic versus emotion, which he says is wrong) but in how they attend to the world. The left hemisphere gives narrow, focused attention for grasping and using: its world is made of static, fungible, categorized, explicit fragments, it values utility, money and power, and it is prone to anger, certainty and delusion. The right gives broad, sustained, vigilant attention: its world is uncertain, interconnected, embodied and implicit, and it grasps the whole and the values of connection, truth, goodness, beauty and love. The left is 'a very good servant, but a very poor master'; McGilchrist argues Western civilization has three times flourished in balance and declined as the left took over, and that modern culture is left-dominated. Evolutionary origin: a bird must focus on a seed while watching for predators."@en .

tgs:Concept.DominationPartnershipContinuum thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RianeEisler ;
    skos:note "In TGS_116 Eisler says the inherited categories -- right and left, religious and secular, Eastern and Western, Northern and Southern, capitalist and socialist -- 'fragment our consciousness' and hide a deeper pattern. Hitler's Germany, Stalin's Soviet Union, the Taliban, Iran and Putin's Russia differ on the surface but share one configuration: authoritarian, punitive family and state, rigid gender stereotypes, and violence ('man over man, man over woman, race over race, religion over religion, nation over nation'). North Korea is socialist and pure domination; the Nordic nations have market economies yet lean to partnership. Neither pure end exists: 'it's always a matter of degree.' Partnership keeps hierarchies -- parents, teachers, leaders -- but leaders inspire and empower rather than control, and transparency becomes possible. She dates the turn toward domination to only 5,000 to 10,000 years ago, and says there never was a matriarchy: the opposite of patriarchy is partnership. Her earlier term for a partnership society is 'gylany' (in the transcript, 'Gaelanic')."@en .

tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RobinDunbar ;
    skos:note "Robin Dunbar's proposed cognitive limit (roughly 150) on the number of people with whom one can maintain genuinely stable social relationships, derived from a correlation between primate brain/neocortex size and social group size — used in this graph's source material as evidence for prioritizing depth over breadth in relationships."@en .

tgs:Concept.EarlyAnthropoceneHypothesis thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.WilliamRuddiman .

tgs:Concept.EarnedHope thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.KristineTompkins .

tgs:Concept.EcocentricDevelopmentalWheel thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.BillPlotkin .

tgs:Concept.Ecolate thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.GarrettHardin ;
    skos:note "Garrett Hardin's real, coined term for ecological literacy — a third literacy alongside 'numerate' (knowing numbers) and 'literate' (knowing words and grammar). Being ecolate means habitually asking 'and then what?' of any proposed action, tracing second-, third-, and nth-order consequences beyond the immediate, narrow-boundary benefit. Real, direct source for this show's own recurring 'wide-boundary thinking' vocabulary."@en .

tgs:Concept.EcologicalAwakening thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.BillPlotkin .

tgs:Concept.EcologicalFootprint thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.MathisWackernagel,
        tgs:Persona.WilliamRees ;
    skos:note "A quantitative sustainability metric, originated and co-developed by William Rees and his then-PhD-student Mathis Wackernagel (introduced in their 1996 book 'Our Ecological Footprint'), expressing human demand on ecosystems as the amount of biologically productive land and water area required to produce what a population consumes and absorb its waste — the primary tool used to estimate the extent of humanity's ecological overshoot."@en .

tgs:Concept.EcologicalSecurity thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RodSchoonover .

tgs:Concept.Effectivism thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NateHagens .

tgs:Concept.EliteOverproduction thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.PeterTurchin .

tgs:Concept.EnergyAdditionsNotTransitions thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.JeanBaptisteFressoz ;
    skos:note "The claim that there has never been a true energy transition -- one dominant source replacing another -- only energy additions: each new source (coal, oil, gas, hydro, nuclear, now wind and solar) has been layered on top of earlier ones, whose use has stayed flat or kept rising. Stated directly by Art Berman in TGS-237 ('there never has been an energy transition... we never transitioned from anything to anything'), with the supporting evidence the show notes attach: traditional biomass use is roughly double its 1800 level (Art said about the same) and coal use is at record highs globally. Berman's practical point is about mindset: the investment world assumes substitution, for which there is no historical precedent -- which does not make it impossible, only low-probability, especially in the near term. Scholarly basis: tgs:Work.EnergyTransitionsOrAdditions (York and Bell, 2019), which argues that renewable growth has so far expanded total energy production rather than displacing fossil fuels, and that calling it a 'transition' is misleading and could inhibit policy aimed at actually reducing fossil fuel use."@en .

tgs:Concept.EnergyGDPCoupling thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NateHagens .

tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment skos:note "The ratio of energy delivered by an energy source to the energy required to extract/produce it; central to Hagens' argument that the quality, not just quantity, of energy sources matters for what a society can sustain."@en .

tgs:Concept.EnergySymbiosis thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.JeanBaptisteFressoz .

tgs:Concept.EnergyTransitionNarrative thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.JeanBaptisteFressoz .

tgs:Concept.Enoughness thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer .

tgs:Concept.EpigeneticLandscape thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.ConradWaddington .

tgs:Concept.EscalationTrap thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RobertPape .

tgs:Concept.ExosomaticEnergy thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NateHagens .

tgs:Concept.FourCornerstonesOfPartnership thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RianeEisler ;
    skos:note "Eisler's theory of change (TGS_116; the last chapter of tgs:Work.NurturingOurHumanity): short-term tactics are not enough without long-term strategies aimed at the four cornerstones. Childhood and family come first, because the first five years shape the brain, and a child in a domination family learns that violence by the stronger against the weaker is normal and moral, and learns the hidden system of gendered values; her first priority is ending violence against children in families, backed by authoritative rather than authoritarian parenting. Gender is 'a primary social, economic, and family organizing principle,' not a women's issue: as the status of women rises, so does the status of values coded feminine, such as caring and nonviolence, so men vote for caring policies too. Economics must value care (tgs:Concept.CaringEconomics). Story and language must change: original sin and the selfish gene are 'the same story' -- we are bad, so we must be controlled from the top. She notes that those pushing back against partnership invest heavily in exactly these long-term strategies -- family control, rigid gender roles."@en .

tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s skos:note "Nate's own real, direct, named term for four real, converging risk categories he's tracked across recent years: financial overshoot, geopolitical shifts, supply chain fragility from complexity unraveling, and the erosion of social contracts. First named directly in Frankly-134, in the real, direct context of the 2026 Iran war, as a real, explicit framework for anticipating which 'seemingly insignificant' single point of failure (his own real example: the black paint shortage that shut down an entire Ford plant after the Fukushima earthquake) might be the next unexpected complexity-driven disruption."@en,
        "Nate's own real, recurring framework, its name a real, direct allusion to the biblical Four Horsemen of the Apocalypse (Conquest/Pestilence, War, Famine, and Death), naming several simultaneous, structurally connected challenges that must be navigated even if any single one (including AI) were somehow solved. Nate's own count varies across episodes — stated as four in Frankly_83 (financial overshoot, geopolitics/war, the social contract, and complexity) and expanded to five in Frankly_92 (debt, ecological/resource overshoot, geopolitics, the zero-sum game that emerges once global economic growth stalls, and polarization/breakdown of the social contract alongside accelerating ecological breakdown)."@en .

tgs:Concept.FreedomToBeRadicallyHuman thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.IndyJohar .

tgs:Concept.FutureOfBeingHuman thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.IndyJohar .

tgs:Concept.GhostOfDopaminePast skos:note "Nate's term (Frankly_103) for the way our recent and distant past behavior carves neural pathways that require ever-higher baseline dopamine to feel normal. Because dopamine signals reward prediction error (tgs:Concept.RewardPredictionError), a life of constant high stimulation -- notifications, metrics, games -- raises the expected reward, and quiet, ancestrally normal experiences (a doe and fawn on the road, half an hour on a park bench watching birds, reading a book) no longer register. A hungry ghost of dopamine past produces an over-consumptive 'ghost of dopamine present' and narrows the 'ghost of dopamine future': we can only imagine futures that keep us on the high treadmill. Remedies he offers: dopamine fasting, a pause between trigger and response, lowering the stimulation baseline (grayscale phones), and socializing the dopamine."@en .

tgs:Concept.GiftEconomy thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer .

tgs:Concept.GlacierStabilization thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.KellyErhart .

tgs:Concept.GlobalGovernanceWithoutGlobalGovernment thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.GoldilocksTechnology skos:note "Nate's own real, coined term for technology calibrated between two failure modes: not too 'hot' (dopaminergic, resource-intensive gadgets that deepen dependence on non-renewable inputs) and not too 'cold' (a naive retreat to stone-age tooling that ignores real human problem-solving capacity). First laid out as a preliminary checklist in Frankly_69, considering real governance incentives, true-cost pricing of externalities, material and supply-chain constraints, and a technology's actual relationship to the biosphere."@en .

tgs:Concept.GrammarOfAnimacy thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer .

tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification thinkr:relatesTo tgs:SchoolOfThought.TechnoOptimism .

tgs:Concept.GreatTurning thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.JoannaMacy ;
    skos:note "Joanna Macy's framework, explained by Plotkin in real depth (not a passing citation): the shift from an 'Industrial Growth Society' toward a 'life-sustaining civilization,' unfolding across three simultaneous dimensions — (1) holding actions to save as much life as possible now, (2) building the structural and technical foundations for healthier systems (food, energy, governance), and (3) a shift in consciousness and values, which Plotkin explicitly connects back to tgs:Concept.EcologicalAwakening and his own developmental wheel. Macy pairs this with 'the Great Unraveling' — the ongoing collapse of existing systems — as two faces of the same real historical moment, not opposed processes."@en .

tgs:Concept.HeadHeartAndHands thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NateHagens .

tgs:Concept.HerosJourney thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.JosephCampbell .

tgs:Concept.HighTouchGrounding thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.HoloceneClimateStability thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NateHagens .

tgs:Concept.HonorableHarvest thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer .

tgs:Concept.HumanExceptionalism thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.ChristineWebb,
        tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer .

tgs:Concept.IAmBecauseWeAre thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife skos:note "Nate's own real, direct phrase, delivered in full definitional form in Frankly_92, distinguishing genuine service to Life (capital L — the full, deep-time continuity of complex life on Earth) from 'in service of life' in the lowercase sense (well-intentioned initiatives like wind farms, recycling, or the UN Sustainable Development Goals, which genuinely help but operate at a fundamentally smaller threshold). Rather than define capital-L service directly, Nate offers ten real, direct criteria for what is NOT in service of Life: (1) anything anthropocentric, caring only about human life; (2) anything reductionist, counting species without accounting for symbiosis and biocomplexity; (3) anything favoring simple life over complex life as though they were equivalent; (4) anything current-focused only, ignoring deep time (most climate models stop at 2099); (5) anything land-only-based, ignoring that oceans hold 70% of Earth's living habitat; (6) anything exoplanetary-focused (Mars colonization, immortal robot bodies, mind uploading) that writes off life on Earth itself; (7) anything causing irreversible species extinction; (8) anything allowing existential global threats (nuclear weapons) to continue; (9) interventions that fix one anthropocentric harm while worsening ecological harm overall (curing malaria without addressing the resulting pressure on rainforest habitat); and (10) growing the economic Superorganism itself, as opposed to slowing or shrinking its metabolism."@en .

tgs:Concept.IndustrialEraEducation thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RosaVasquezEspinoza .

tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger,
        tgs:Persona.MarvinHarris ;
    skos:note "Daniel Schmachtenberger's three-layer model of a civilization, used in TGS_132 to show why technology alone cannot solve climate change. Infrastructure is the technology (efficient machines, renewable energy); social structure is law and economic incentives; superstructure is culture -- what people value, which is the basis of what can be codified into law. In the episode he points out the move he and Nate have just made: starting with technical fixes, realizing they require changes to law and incentives, then realizing those require changes in values. 'It takes superstructure, social structure and infrastructure altogether'; technology 'only plays a meaningful role as a part of that integrated suite.'"@en .

tgs:Concept.InsectDecline thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.OliverMilman .

tgs:Concept.InstitutionalDarkMatter thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.IndyJohar .

tgs:Concept.IntelligenceBoundByWisdom thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger,
        tgs:Persona.IainMcGilchrist .

tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.IlyaPrigogine ;
    skos:note "Ilya Prigogine's real concept, directly and repeatedly paraphrased by Nate across multiple episodes: when a complex system is far from equilibrium, small 'islands of coherence' within it have the capacity to shift the entire system to a higher order. Used throughout this show as a direct argument for the real, disproportionate potential impact of small, genuinely coherent groups or individuals during periods of systemic instability. Real, direct disambiguation added 2026-09-16: NOT the same real concept as tgs:Concept.IslandsOfSanity (Margaret Wheatley's own distinct term, named directly in TGS-234) -- related in spirit, different real originators and frames; kept as two separate Concepts rather than merged."@en .

tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.WilliamStanleyJevons .

tgs:Concept.KinshipWorldview thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer .

tgs:Concept.KleibersLaw thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.MaxKleiber ;
    skos:note "Max Kleiber's 1932 empirical finding that an organism's basal metabolic rate scales to roughly the 3/4 power of its body mass, not the 2/3 power a simple surface-area argument would predict — one of the few genuine quantitative laws in biology, still without a fully settled theoretical explanation, and used in this graph's own source material as a real biological analogy for economic/systemic energy-scaling arguments."@en .

tgs:Concept.LandscapeOfPossibleFutures thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.ConradWaddington ;
    skos:note "Hagens' own application of the landscape metaphor (valleys, ridges, switchbacks, erosion) to civilizational futures — see tgs:Monologue.Frankly_145_Part3UphillFutures for the full framework. Adapts Conrad Waddington's epigenetic landscape (originally a model of cellular differentiation) to a different domain entirely — a genuine reframing, not a direct reuse, hence HagensConcept despite the explicit external inspiration."@en .

tgs:Concept.LeveragePoints thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DonellaMeadows .

tgs:Concept.LifeEnnoblingEconomics thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.IndyJohar .

tgs:Concept.LocalizedSyntropySystemicEntropy thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.LogicOfSeparation thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.LootableSurplus thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.LukeKemp .

tgs:Concept.MaximizeImpactNotMinimizeFootprint thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NateHagens,
        tgs:Persona.RutgerBregman ;
    skos:note "Nate's maxim to his students, 'maximize your impact as opposed to minimize your consumption': if everyone lived at the smallest possible ecological footprint, no one would change the larger system driving the crises. Rutger Bregman (TGS_195) endorses it, calling personal footprint rules (no meat, no flying, electric cars, tiny houses) 'the moral minimum' or 'human hygiene': if done perfectly, 'you might as well not have existed', so death becomes the highest ideal. He contrasts checklist approaches such as B Corp certification with the 'sky is the limit' ambition of Silicon Valley founders, which he wants pointed at pandemics, climate breakdown and poverty."@en .

tgs:Concept.Metacrisis thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.GregoryBateson .

tgs:Concept.MigrationAsHumanNorm thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.SunilAmrith .

tgs:Concept.MoneyAsClaimOnEnergy thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.Aristotle .

tgs:Concept.MoralAmbition thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RutgerBregman .

tgs:Concept.MultilevelSelection thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DavidSloanWilson ;
    skos:note "The evolutionary theory that natural selection acts at several levels at once: within a group the selfish individual outcompetes others, but between groups the group with the fewest selfish individuals wins, so selfishness at one level undermines welfare at the next level up. As Josh Farley sets it out in TGS_185, it runs through the major evolutionary transitions in which once-independent units became so interdependent they could survive only as a collective: archaea and bacteria into the eukaryotic cell, cells into multicellular animals, animals into social species. A mitochondrion or cell that turns selfish is disease or cancer; a human who pursues only self-interest harms society; and for humans the decisive group-level selection is cultural. Farley's conclusion is that the individual should relate to society, and humanity to nature, as a cell relates to the body."@en .

tgs:Concept.MusicalChairsMoment thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NateHagens .

tgs:Concept.NaiveProgress thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.NarrowGoalAchieving thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.NatureBasedPackaging thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.WesCarter .

tgs:Concept.OceanAlkalinityEnhancement thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.KellyErhart .

tgs:Concept.OmniLose thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.OpportunityRiskAsymmetry thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:note "Daniel Schmachtenberger's account (TGS_71) of why risky technology is deployed faster than it is understood. Those who focus on a technology's opportunity move faster than those who do careful safety analysis; they gain first-mover advantage, network effects, revenue, lobbying power and public influence, while those who take the risks seriously either decide not to build (and gain no power to affect the system) or spend their resources on safety instead of scale. Limited liability lets corporations privatize gains and socialize losses, and 'unknown unknowns' become 'a source of bullshit plausible deniability' for not even trying to forecast externalities. 'It is a market function... forever,' but the stakes now rise with exponential technology, making this asymmetry 'itself one of the underlying drivers of the meta crisis.'"@en .

tgs:Concept.OriginalTripleInformationHazard skos:note "Nate's own real, direct, original formulation (Frankly-34, June 2023), later explicitly grown into tgs:Concept.TwelveInformationHazards (Frankly-153, 2026). The real, original three: (1) things Nate knows but can't say publicly, because the knowing itself would make the risk self-fulfilling -- maps most directly onto the later list's self-fulfilling-prophecy hazard. (2) A real, direct truth-to-power hazard: describing the erosion of US global power was safe while 'the global tide was rising and lifting all boats,' but becomes genuinely threatening to those in power as that tide recedes -- named identically, by the same real term, in the later twelve-part list. (3) A real, direct, distinct hazard the later list calls the spotlight hazard: Nate has met people doing real, quietly effective conservation and social-finance work in Africa and elsewhere who explicitly don't want their own work publicized, since public awareness would short-circuit its own success -- meaning some genuinely good news can't be shared any more freely than bad news can."@en .

tgs:Concept.OstromDesignPrinciples thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons,
        tgs:Persona.ElinorOstrom ;
    skos:note "Ostrom's empirical answer to Hardin (tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons). Hardin's model assumes strangers with open access and no rules; real commons usually have governance, and users who meet again and again can build and enforce it. Her field studies found the same eight features in commons that lasted for generations: (1) clearly defined boundaries of who may use what; (2) rules congruent with local conditions; (3) collective-choice arrangements, so those bound by the rules can change them; (4) monitoring, usually by the users themselves; (5) graduated sanctions, mild for a first lapse and escalating for repeat defection; (6) accessible, low-cost conflict resolution; (7) recognition by outside authorities of the users' right to organize; (8) nested enterprises, with local rules for local matters and higher tiers for coordination across communities. Commons governance tends to fail where groups are too large or heterogeneous for reputation and repeated dealings to work, where outside authorities override local rules or grant access to outsiders (colonial regimes often created open access where none existed), and where resources change faster than rules can adapt. Hardin later accepted the narrowing: his argument applies to unmanaged, open-access commons."@en .

tgs:Concept.Overshoot thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.WilliamCatton ;
    skos:note "The condition of a population/economy consuming renewable and non-renewable resources faster than natural systems can regenerate or absorb waste from them, borrowed from systems ecology (Catton, Meadows) and applied to the global economy."@en .

tgs:Concept.PeakOil thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.MKingHubbert ;
    skos:note "The point at which the maximum rate of petroleum extraction is reached, after which production enters terminal decline — a formative concept in Hagens' background (he worked as a peak oil analyst before founding The Great Simplification) and foundational to his energy-decline framing."@en .

tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency skos:note "The lived experience that some part of one's life, attention, and time is genuinely one's own to choose — distinct from tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency (a collective/systemic pattern about who drives change). Frankly_129's own organizing term, scientifically grounded via learned-helplessness/self-efficacy research (the nervous system learning whether effort produces outcomes). Frankly_142 directly ties it to dread: 'dread is the experience of being acted upon by a future you cannot influence, and agency is the opposite stance.'"@en .

tgs:Concept.PhantomCarryingCapacity thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.WilliamCatton .

tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.JohanRockstrom ;
    skos:note "Real, foundational scientific framework led by Johan Rockström and colleagues (originally published 2009), identifying nine distinct Earth-system processes with critical thresholds — climate change, biosphere integrity, land-system change, freshwater use, biogeochemical flows, ocean acidification, atmospheric aerosol loading, stratospheric ozone depletion, and novel entities (chemical pollution) — within which humanity and the biosphere can continue to develop safely. As of the framework's own 2023 update, six of nine boundaries have been exceeded."@en .

tgs:Concept.PositiveSumInGroupNegativeSumToNature thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.PostTragic thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:note "A three-stage model Daniel Schmachtenberger credits to his friend Marc Gafni (TGS_31). Pre-tragic optimism holds ideals not yet shattered by the world -- naivete. Encountering the tragedy of the world shatters them, and cynicism can emerge. Post-tragic is 'committed to being in service to the sacredness of life whether you can succeed or not' -- 'still the right hill to die on' -- and sees that the tragic place has a false certainty just as the naive one did, and that 'the universe is much bigger than both.' It looks for solutions with full cognizance of the tragedy, and also holds the fragility of life on a thin crust beside a sun that can flare. Daniel adds two practical notes: fear and anger at harm are 'epiphenomena' of love for something sacred, so one can act 'more for than against'; and people in pain first need to be met in their grief, since premature optimism can be a 'spiritual bypass,' before asking what agency remains."@en .

tgs:Concept.PowerOfLifeVsPowerOverLife thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.Pragmatopia thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RianeEisler .

tgs:Concept.PredicamentNotProblem skos:note "Nate's own real, direct, recurring distinction, stated plainly in Frankly-133: a problem has solutions; a predicament merely has pathways that are better, even if only slightly better, than other pathways. Real, direct implication he draws from it: the more-than-human predicament cannot be 'solved' in the way a problem can, only navigated toward a real, comparatively better outcome among several imperfect ones."@en .

tgs:Concept.PreemptivePeace thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.IndyJohar .

tgs:Concept.Presence skos:note "Frankly_143's own central subject: full attentional engagement with the present moment, framed as the destination dread transforms into 'when fully metabolized' (per Frankly_142's own closing) and as the ground from which any real response to the metacrisis must be taken."@en .

tgs:Concept.ProblemSolvingAsRacket thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.ProgressNarrativeAsApologism thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.PunishmentStrategy thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RobertPape .

tgs:Concept.ReAgentificationOfTheWorld thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.IndyJohar .

tgs:Concept.RealityParty thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NateHagens .

tgs:Concept.Rebuildables skos:note "Hagens' own reframing of 'renewable energy' infrastructure (solar panels, wind turbines, batteries): since manufacturing, installing, and eventually replacing this infrastructure depends on fossil-fuel-powered mining, transport, and industrial processes at every stage, he argues 'rebuildable' is a more honest description than 'renewable,' which implies a self-sustaining energy source independent of the fossil fuel base it currently depends on."@en .

tgs:Concept.RecursiveAbstraction thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.RedQueenEffect skos:note "A real, direct term coined for oil-depletion economics (Frankly-109 credits 'Rune Likvern' and The Oil Drum, circa 2012), borrowed from the Red Queen's own real line to Alice in 'Through the Looking-Glass' -- 'it takes all the running you can do, to keep in the same place.' Real, direct application: as existing oil wells decline, ever-more new wells must be drilled just to hold total production flat, let alone grow it -- shown directly via stacked charts of each year's new-well production layered on top of the accumulating decline of all prior years' wells. The same real, direct dynamic is noted as applying to natural gas as well."@en .

tgs:Concept.RedefiningSuccess thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RutgerBregman .

tgs:Concept.RegenerativeFinance thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.MatthewMonahan .

tgs:Concept.RelativeFitnessSpiteFramework skos:note "Nate's own real, structured four-quadrant behavioral framework, grounded in the evolutionary distinction between relative fitness (reproductive success measured against competitors, the real evolutionary currency) and absolute fitness (raw offspring count or physical condition alone). The four quadrants: selfishness (cost to a competitor, benefit to self), cooperation (mutual benefit), altruism (cost to self, benefit to a genetically unrelated other — rare in nature, though reciprocal altruism is common in humans), and spite (a real, direct cost to oneself, provided it imposes a greater cost on a competitor)."@en .

tgs:Concept.RestorationAndRestoryation thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer .

tgs:Concept.RewardPredictionError thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.WolframSchultz .

tgs:Concept.Rewilding thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.KristineTompkins .

tgs:Concept.RewritingTheRulesOfTheSuperorganism thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RodSchoonover .

tgs:Concept.RightsOfNature skos:note "The legal and ethical movement to recognize ecosystems, species or natural features (rivers, mountains, whales) as holders of rights or legal persons, rather than property. In TGS_226 Matthew Monahan highlights two mountains in Ma Earth's third funding round: Taranaki Maunga in Aotearoa New Zealand, granted personhood through a treaty settlement between the Crown and Māori, which can steward its own funds; and Pyramid Mountain, the first US mountain to own itself, governed through a guardianship council, along with rights-of-whales initiatives. The show notes link it to Nate's earlier episode with Pella Thiel on ecocide law and the rights of nature."@en .

tgs:Concept.RiskSingularity thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.RobustnessVsPerformance skos:note "Olivier Hamant's own real, central, defining distinction (TGS-230): performance is efficacy plus efficiency -- reaching a goal with the least means; robustness is a system's capacity to remain stable and viable despite fluctuation. Real, direct argument: evolution does not select for the most efficient organism, it selects for the most robust one -- species surviving millions of years have to withstand fluctuation long before they can afford to specialize. Human civilization inverted this: cheap, abundant fossil energy substituted an 'abundance of resources' safety net for the real safety net living systems actually use (diversity, redundancy, cooperation), producing what Hamant calls, borrowing Charles Perrow's real term, a 'normal accident' -- a system so complex and interdependent that failure becomes inevitable, not exceptional. Real, direct four determinants of robustness Hamant names explicitly: redundancy (a real plan B), heterogeneity (plan B genuinely different from plan A, not a duplicate), link to the living (dependence on local, living systems rather than only extracted minerals), and deep link to the local territory (social, economic, and material embeddedness in a specific place). Real, direct three-principle synthesis, in Hamant's own words the actual root lesson underneath all three: circularity, cooperation, and robustness itself -- explicitly named as prerequisites for navigating the 21st century 'with joy,' not mere survival tactics."@en .

tgs:Concept.RocksInTheRiver skos:note "Nate's own real, direct metaphor, originating in Frankly-88 ('Snow, the Singularity, and Rocks in the River') and independently confirmed by RR-26's own real citation of it months later: rather than trying to reform the current system directly, cultivate a small, real number of psychologically prepared, grounded individuals within communities who can act as stabilizing anchors -- literal rocks -- slowing the current as broader systems destabilize around them, allowing new capacity to accumulate in their lee the way sediment gathers behind a real rock in a real river. Directly recurs in Frankly-130 as Nate's own closing description of where the platform's actual, real work is now shifting -- local coordination and ecological preparedness, not further wide-boundary commentary."@en .

tgs:Concept.ScenarioThinking skos:note "Deliberately holding multiple plausible futures at once rather than committing to a single predicted storyline — the organizing methodology behind the entire 'How to Think About the Future' series (see tgs:Series.HowToThinkAboutTheFuture and tgs:Scenario) and explicitly contrasted by Hagens with forecasting."@en .

tgs:Concept.Schismogenesis thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.GregoryBateson ;
    skos:note "Gregory Bateson's term (from his 1930s anthropological fieldwork) for a process in which relationships escalate through cumulative interaction — either symmetrically (both parties responding to each other's same behavior with more of the same, e.g. an arms race) or complementarily (parties responding with opposite behaviors that reinforce each other, e.g. dominance and submission), each pattern intensifying over time unless deliberately interrupted."@en .

tgs:Concept.SecondPrecisionRevolution thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RobertPape .

tgs:Concept.SevenFiresProphecy thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer .

tgs:Concept.SevenGenerationsThinking thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer .

tgs:Concept.SevenKeyInterventions thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NateHagens .

tgs:Concept.ShiftingBaselineSyndrome thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NateHagens .

tgs:Concept.ShortfallRisk skos:note "A framing Hagens borrows from portfolio management/finance and repurposes for civilizational risk assessment: the danger that something essential (topsoil, social trust, grid stability, the nuclear-use taboo, an aquifer) drops below a threshold it cannot easily recover from — distinct from more commonly discussed AVERAGE-outcome risk, and argued to be the more important lens for genuinely existential threats specifically because average-case thinking systematically undercounts them."@en .

tgs:Concept.Sobriety thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.JeanMarcJancovici .

tgs:Concept.SocialLimitsToGrowthBeforePhysical thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NateHagens,
        tgs:Persona.PeterTurchin .

tgs:Concept.SolarGeoengineeringGovernance thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.TedParson .

tgs:Concept.SolarRadiationModification thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.TedParson ;
    skos:note "Cooling the planet by reflecting more sunlight back to space, most studied as stratospheric aerosol injection of sulfates, mimicking volcanic eruptions such as Mount Pinatubo. The idea was first raised in a 1965 report to President Lyndon Johnson. Kelly Erhart (TGS_204) calls it the only researched emergency brake for runaway warming, not a solution: it does not address the causes, its regional effects are poorly bounded, and it is at best an insurance policy or a way to buy time. She rejects the 'rogue billionaire' scenario as convenient but incorrect, since sustained deployment would take a nation state, and argues that governance (who decides, who bears the regional consequences) requires research first, especially by Global South researchers. Ted Parson, interviewed by Nate the day before, puts the chance of eventual deployment at 90%."@en .

tgs:Concept.SpaceOfTheNecessary thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.IndyJohar .

tgs:Concept.StrawSiphonSieve skos:note "Nate's own real, direct three-part model (Frankly-119, extending Frankly-56's own earlier straw metaphor) for what technology actually does to real wealth -- defined here explicitly as usable energy, organized matter, and the stocks and flows that make life possible, not money, which is only a real, direct claim on that wealth. (1) The straw: technology increases the rate at which stocks of the natural world are drawn down -- his own real, direct food-system example, the Haber-Bosch process, widened the nitrogen straw so dramatically that roughly half the nitrogen in a human body today originates from fossil fuels. (2) The siphon: gains from that faster extraction concentrate via real, structural network effects (capital rewards those who can finance it, data compounds, early advantage reinforces itself) and via real, direct choke points -- narrow gates (ports, app stores, platforms) where the toolmaker becomes the rent-taker without needing to produce more value, just control access to it. (3) The sieve: technology filters long-term wealth away from the rest of life and toward one species, and within that species toward a narrow subset -- humans now consume roughly 40% of Earth's net primary productivity, and the built technosphere now outweighs all living biomass combined. Real, direct closing extension: debt is named directly as a fourth, social form of the same pattern (a straw pulling future resources into the present, a siphon concentrating returns to those who can issue credit), and AI is analyzed as the newest technology running through this same real, direct three-part structure, but in the cognitive rather than physical economy."@en .

tgs:Concept.SuccessAsLongTermPersistence thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RobinWallKimmerer .

tgs:Concept.SuperorganismFreeZones thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NateHagens .

tgs:Concept.SupplyChainFragility thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.EdConway ;
    skos:note "Modern global supply chains are far more concentrated, specialized, and fragile than they appear — a small number of hyper-specialized, often invisible 'pinch points' (a single factory, a single region, a single material) can be genuinely load-bearing for enormous downstream systems, disproportionate to their own small economic footprint. Real evidenced cases: the 1915 UK 'Glass Famine' (an entire domestic industry allowed to wither, then found existentially necessary within a few years, per tgs:Interview.TGS_127_EdConway); a single Birmingham factory supplying roughly half the world's automotive rearview-mirror electrodes; the 2011 Fukushima earthquake halting Ford's US truck production over a single specialized paint pigment. The mechanism Ed Conway identifies: modern comparative-advantage-driven specialization increases aggregate efficiency while increasing structural fragility — 'you have these factories which are uber-specialised... and they're pinch points. Until those pinch points explode, you don't really know about it.'"@en .

tgs:Concept.Symmathesy thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NoraBateson .

tgs:Concept.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Organization.ConsilienceProject,
        tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:note "The thesis of a Consilience Project paper, explained by Daniel Schmachtenberger in TGS_71: tools are not neutral instruments of pre-existing values. A technology that confers competitive advantage becomes obligate, because whoever does not adopt it (or something comparable) loses in rivalrous interactions; and using a tool encodes new patterns of behavior that change individual minds and whole societies. His illustration is the plow: animistic cultures that would not castrate and whip oxen, holding a belief in the spirit of the buffalo, could not produce the caloric surplus of those that did, and lost. 'The tools become obligate... the tools code different patterns of human behavior... psychology affects the tools we create, but the tools in turn affect the psychologies.' Undirected, that feedback runs downhill toward evolutionary cul-de-sacs."@en .

tgs:Concept.TechnologyJengaTower thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.BalazsMatics .

tgs:Concept.TheSenecaEffect thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.UgoBardi .

tgs:Concept.ThingsOfValueThroughTheBottlenecks thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DJWhite,
        tgs:Persona.NateHagens .

tgs:Concept.ThirdAttractor thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.ThreeCirclesOfAIEnvironmentalImpact thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.TwoCategoriesOfTechnology thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NateHagens .

tgs:Concept.TwoWaysOfKnowing thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.RosaVasquezEspinoza .

tgs:Concept.UneconomicGrowth thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.HermanDaly .

tgs:Concept.Untax thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NateHagens .

tgs:Concept.WarAndTheClimateCrisis thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.SunilAmrith .

tgs:Concept.WarmingInducedEmissions thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.KellyErhart .

tgs:Concept.WetBulbTemperature skos:note "A single measure combining heat and humidity: the lowest temperature to which air can be cooled by evaporating water into it, which is how hot conditions feel to a body cooling itself by sweating. The higher the dew point, the more moisture in the air and the harder it is for sweat to evaporate. At a sustained wet-bulb temperature of about 35 C (95 F), humans can no longer cool themselves even in shade with a breeze and unlimited water, which is why rising dew points and wet-bulb events are a growing concern in a warming world."@en .

tgs:Concept.Wetware thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.GameTheory,
        tgs:Persona.SigmundFreud .

tgs:Concept.WhatWeDesignDesignsUsBack thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.IndyJohar .

tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySovereignty thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NateHagens .

tgs:Concept.WileECoyoteMoment thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.NateHagens .

