tgs:Concept.CorporationAsObligateSociopath thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.CorporationsAsMisalignedAI,
        tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.CorporationsAsMisalignedAI thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.CorporationAsObligateSociopath ;
    skos:note "Nate's own real, direct, striking reframe (Frankly-159), offered in response to being asked why he doesn't prioritize AI alignment risk as one of his own four core categories of work: humanity has already lived for well over a century with a real, direct non-alignment problem between human/biospheric good and the actual behavior of 'soulless, mindless, immortal automatons composed of multiple collaborating agents' -- legally-recognized persons in the US, unlike AI, which is not (yet) a legal person anywhere. The real, direct argument: the hypothetical future problem of a superintelligent AI misaligned with human interests is something humanity is already living through today, just under a different name -- business as usual."@en .

tgs:Concept.CulturalAntibodies thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.PrematureCertainty,
        tgs:Persona.NateHagens .

tgs:Concept.DarkTriad thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.LightTriad ;
    skos:note "A cluster of three socially aversive personality traits — narcissism, Machiavellianism, and psychopathy — originally proposed by Delroy Paulhus and Kevin Williams (2002). A recurring analytical lens across this graph's source material for understanding how individuals with these traits gain disproportionate influence over institutions and culture, distinct from mere individual pathology."@en .

tgs:Concept.DiscretePhaseShift thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.ImaginalCells,
        tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.DopamineHijackedCulture thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.OneMarshmallowRewardCircuits,
        tgs:Persona.NateHagens .

tgs:Concept.EcologyOfTheoriesOfChange thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.TheoryOfChange,
        tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.EmbeddedGrowthObligation thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative,
        tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:note "Daniel Schmachtenberger's term for a system that must keep growing to keep functioning -- 'macro and micro.' At civilizational scale, the global economy carries an exponential embedded growth obligation, and with it exponentially growing externalities, against planetary boundaries (TGS_5, TGS_20, TGS_42, TGS_50, TGS_71, TGS_132). At personal scale (TGS_50), people create their own: each raise becomes a bigger house, more things and more debt, requiring more income. His advice, especially to the young: keep overhead low, earn what you need in as few hours as possible doing something 'that isn't terrible,' and spend the time bought back developing your capacity to serve -- 'you can't be growing your service to the existing world maximally at the same time.'"@en .

