tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.MaximizingReturnsOnPower,
        tgs:Persona.HowardOdum ;
    skos:note "Howard T. Odum's principle (building on Alfred Lotka's earlier work) that self-organizing systems tend to develop and prevail when they maximize their intake and useful transformation of energy, not merely their efficiency — a foundational idea in ecological energetics directly connected to EROI (Charles Hall, a notable Odum student, is a central figure in that literature too)."@en .

tgs:Concept.MetabolicHierarchyOfTheSuperorganism thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.MaximizingReturnsOnPower,
        tgs:Persona.NateHagens .

tgs:Concept.MoneyAsMaximumOptionality thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.ThreeTiersOfWealth,
        tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger .

tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.Superorganism,
        tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:note "A coordination failure in which many competing actors each act rationally in their own interest and together produce an outcome that is worse for all of them, while no single actor can step out without being outcompeted. Nate's plain-language version (Frankly_160): if all of nearly two hundred nations used less energy together, all would benefit, but a country that restrains itself alone falls behind economically and militarily, and its leaders are replaced by someone promising growth -- 'What is best for all of us is not what is best for each of us.' A widely used term in TGS conversations about the metacrisis, closely related to the collective action problem and the tragedy of the commons."@en .

