tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.GarrettHardin ;
    skos:note "Garrett Hardin's 1968 argument (tgs:Work.TragedyOfTheCommons1968) that a shared, unregulated resource tends toward overexploitation: each user captures the full gain of using a little more, while the cost is spread across everyone, so rational individual choices together exhaust the commons unless some form of restraint or governance binds them. In Daniel Schmachtenberger's framework (TGS_20) it is one of three kinds of multipolar trap, alongside the arms race and the market race to first-mover advantage: 'if I don't cut the tree down... somebody else cuts the tree down,' and uses the timber's advantage against me. His central example is a carbon tax: if one country taxes carbon and its rivals do not, it does not prevent climate change, it only cedes power -- 'unless everybody can, nobody can.'"@en .

