tgs:Concept.FragmentedConsciousness thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.ShadowOfTheSuperorganism,
        tgs:Concept.VerticalBehavioralStack,
        tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:note "David Bohm's diagnosis, adopted by Daniel Schmachtenberger (TGS_31): the fundamental cause of all the world's problems, from environmental destruction and war to family conflict, is a fragmented consciousness that does not perceive from wholeness first. Daniel joins it to Einstein's 'optical delusion of consciousness': distinct things are taken to be separable, though nothing exists without the sun, the biosphere and everything else. From that delusion we optimize one part at the expense of another -- unknowingly ('mistake theory,' externalities) or deliberately ('conflict theory,' war and oppression) -- whether the trade is against our future self (addiction), another part of ourselves, another person, our own interests (martyrdom), another in-group, or the biosphere. Seen whole, all are 'short-term pump and dumps.' Daniel calls this 'the generator function of the generator function.' Reductionism enacts it institutionally, converting 'the complex to the complicated' -- hence iatrogenic medicine and environmental fixes that move problems elsewhere."@en .

