tgs:Concept.PrecautionaryPrinciple thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:note "The principle that when an action carries significant uncertainty and a non-trivial possibility of irreversible, catastrophic harm, the burden of proof falls on proving it safe before it proceeds, rather than on proving harm afterwards. Daniel Schmachtenberger applies it to AI in TGS_132: today the order is reversed -- lead went into gasoline and was regulated only 'after a billion IQ points are gone' -- and with technology far more powerful and faster-scaling, as planetary boundaries are crossed, harms of that kind cannot be reversed. 'If we do not get that, I don't think we make it.'"@en .

tgs:Concept.ThreeByThreeResponseGrid thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.SolutionSpaceCube,
        tgs:Concept.ThreeTimelinesOfResponse,
        tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:note "Daniel Schmachtenberger's framework (Bend Not Break Part 5, TGS_50) for organizing responses to the metacrisis: three layers -- culture, political economy and technology -- against three time horizons. TRIAGE: non-systemic, direct action against a specific catastrophic risk in the near term, recruiting whatever forces are large enough, for whatever reason they care (protecting the Amazon's hydrological pump by showing the US State Department it is a food-security threat; regulating risky synthetic-biology research). TRANSITION: systemic change within existing systems, making institutions smarter and less perverse (a bundled deal for Ecuador combining debt relief, a development plan and exclusive protection that binds out the mining and logging contracts; Larry Lessig's reforms of partisan primaries, ranked-choice voting, gerrymandering and campaign finance). LONG-TERM: building fundamentally new systems (real-cost valuation of everything, education that changes what people value, closed-loop food and manufacturing, governance able to assess exponential technology before deployment). All three must start now and inform each other, or transition moves 'sideways rather than forward' and long-term thinking stays too abstract; in triage the three layers are hard to separate."@en .

