tgs:SchoolOfThought.DegrowthMovement thinkr:relatesTo tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheGreatSimplification .

tgs:Concept.GameTheoreticVersusRealValue thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.GameTheory,
        tgs:Concept.HavesAndHaveNots,
        tgs:Concept.PriceCostAndValue,
        tgs:Concept.TransmutingWealthIntoIncome,
        tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:note "Daniel Schmachtenberger's distinction (TGS_42): 'game theoretical value and real value have a major delta.' We value breathing but do not pay for the atmosphere, so cutting down trees makes sense to each individual -- the lumber is immediate, the damage to the air is imperceptible -- even though everyone doing it threatens the atmosphere as a whole. Anything abundant stays out of the accounting system and is debased. Money therefore flows not to what we value but to the subset that can be extracted and exchanged for competitive advantage; whoever spends to protect the unpriced loses ground to whoever spends only to make more money. The consequence for the transition: what is needed lowers immediate game-theoretic power relative to others, but need not lower quality of life, and could raise it for everyone at once 'so long as we get off of that coordination failure calculus.' 'The definition of best self-interest game theoretically and the definition of best self-interest in any other meaningful way are not the same.'"@en .

tgs:Concept.HyperAgents thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.EducatingHyperAgents,
        tgs:Concept.TheFifteenHundred,
        tgs:Concept.TheLastCanToKick,
        tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:note "Daniel Schmachtenberger's theory (Bend Not Break Part 2, TGS_20; then a Consilience Project paper in progress) of the people at the top of a power-law distribution who have outsized influence on the world -- the Genghis Khans and Alexanders. They lie outside the bell curve: better at game theory and war, more competitive, generally more sociopathic. Their defining trait is maximizing returns on agency -- using agency to grow agency -- and the most capable (tier one) can do it at scale across finance, politics, technology, culture and human motivation. They create the topologies -- incentive and deterrent landscapes and cultures -- in which everyone else forms values. Agency is not the same as personal wealth (Kissinger, or Xi Jinping stewarding China's GDP). Many people are hyper-agents in motivation but not in skill, from middle managers to street thugs. Hyper-agents are themselves caught in a multipolar trap: whoever spends on what they value instead of maximizing agency loses to whoever does not, so even 'the most ethical hyper-agent' feels obligated to seek maximum power. They also convert the intrinsically motivated work of others into game-theoretic power: Einstein's physics into the bomb, Jesus's teaching into Constantine's Christendom."@en .

tgs:Concept.ImpossibleToHard thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.AggregateProbability,
        tgs:Concept.NamedSolutionsDrawCounterResponse,
